From 44510f18d3d29ce79b75c20384ebc58b05d38b1d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ElXreno <elxreno@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 10:24:42 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: baytrail: Clear IRQ triggers on pins muxed to a
 native function

byt_init_irq_valid_mask() clears the trigger bits only on pins whose
mux selects GPIO mode. Firmware may leave a trigger enabled on a pin
that runs a native function, and the trigger block still latches the
pin's bit in INT_STAT then. No GPIO IRQ is mapped for such a pin, so
nothing ever acks the bit, and byt_gpio_irq_handler() is re-entered
for the same hwirq as fast as the IO-APIC line can fire.

On a Prestigio Visconte 2 (Clevo S210TU, Celeron N2806) the firmware
leaves both edge triggers set on SCORE pin 6, PCIE_CLKREQ3, the CLKREQ#
line of the Wi-Fi card. After boot the CPU taking the SCORE interrupt
spends more than half of its time in hardirq context: a 0.3 s kprobe on
generic_handle_domain_irq() logged 22236 calls, all with hwirq 6, and a
CPU-bound job pinned to that CPU took 8.95 s against 3.87 s on the other
one.

Clear the triggers on every pin that does not use direct IRQ mode.
Users that need the interrupt request the pin, which switches it to
GPIO mode and programs the trigger again through irq_set_type().
---
 drivers/pinctrl/intel/pinctrl-baytrail.c | 10 ++++++----
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/pinctrl/intel/pinctrl-baytrail.c b/drivers/pinctrl/intel/pinctrl-baytrail.c
index b733ec3..e92083b 100644
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/intel/pinctrl-baytrail.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/intel/pinctrl-baytrail.c
@@ -1451,9 +1451,11 @@ static void byt_init_irq_valid_mask(struct gpio_chip *chip,
 	int i;
 
 	/*
-	 * Clear interrupt triggers for all pins that are GPIOs and
-	 * do not use direct IRQ mode. This will prevent spurious
-	 * interrupts from misconfigured pins.
+	 * Clear interrupt triggers for all pins that do not use direct
+	 * IRQ mode, including pins muxed to a native function: their
+	 * trigger block still latches INT_STAT, and a latched bit on a
+	 * pin nobody requested is never acked. This will prevent
+	 * spurious interrupts from misconfigured pins.
 	 */
 	for (i = 0; i < vg->soc->npins; i++) {
 		unsigned int pin = vg->soc->pins[i].number;
@@ -1473,7 +1475,7 @@ static void byt_init_irq_valid_mask(struct gpio_chip *chip,
 					   BYT_TRIG_NEG | BYT_TRIG_LVL);
 				writel(value, reg);
 			}
-		} else if ((value & BYT_PIN_MUX) == byt_get_gpio_mux(vg, i)) {
+		} else {
 			byt_gpio_clear_triggering(vg, i);
 			dev_dbg(vg->dev, "disabling GPIO %d\n", i);
 		}
